<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Savaiʻi</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Savaiʻi is the largest island of the Samoan archipelago and of the independent state of Samoa, a shield volcano whose last eruptions, from Mount Matavanu in 1905–1911, buried villages in lava. Its high chiefly titles were among the leading titles of Samoa. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> held it with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upolu">Upolu</a> as German Samoa from 1900 until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> occupied the colony in 1914.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/savaii.jpg" alt="Savaiʻi" width="320" height="197" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Telim tor · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Samoa_Savaii_map.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Savaiʻi is the largest and highest island both in Samoa and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samoan-islands">Samoan Islands</a> chain, and the sixth largest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polynesia">Polynesia</a>. Its classical Samoan name, used in formal oratory and prose, is Salafai. It lies north-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upolu">Upolu</a>, from which it is separated by the Apolima Strait, about 13 km wide, with the small inhabited islands of Manono and Apolima between them. The island is mountainous and fertile and is surrounded by coral reefs, rising to 1,858 metres at Mt Silisili, the highest peak in the country.</p><p>Savaiʻi is the largest shield volcano in the South Pacific, and only 3% of it is above water. Its central region holds the Central Savaiʻi Rainforest, about 72,699 hectares, the largest continuous rainforest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polynesia">Polynesia</a>, dotted with more than 100 volcanic craters. The name is cognate with Hawaiʻi and with Hawaiki in Māori, and shares a root with the original homeland of Polynesian folklore, although Samoans themselves preserve no tradition of having originated elsewhere. The island has been called the Cradle of Polynesia.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Archaeology has uncovered prehistoric settlements inland, including sites at Sapapaliʻi and Vailoa, and star mounds, fortifications and pyramids such as the Pulemelei Mound in Palauli district. In prehistory the village of Safotu was a settlement for Tongans. The missionary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-williams-missionary">John Williams</a> (1796-1839) arrived at Sapapaliʻi in 1830, a village that was also a base for the Malietoa title on Savaiʻi. In October 1839 the island was surveyed by the United States Exploring Expedition under Charles Wilkes.</p><p>The Matavanu eruptions of 1905 to 1911 sent lava over the central north coast and destroyed villages including Saleaula. Resistance to colonial rule began on Savaiʻi: the Mau a Pule movement, led by the exiled orator Lauaki Namulauulu Mamoe (died 1915) of Safotulafai, was the beginning of the Mau, Samoa's non-violent movement for political independence.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The island is home to 43,958 people (2016 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>), 24% of the population of Samoa, and is divided into six political districts. Its only township and ferry terminal is Salelologa. Faʻa Sāmoa, the traditional way of life based on extended family, chiefly titles (matai) and obligations, remains strong there, and the term Pule is applied to traditional authority. Samoa became independent from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> on 1 January 1962. Falealupo, at the westernmost point, lies just 32 km from the dateline, and the 1926 documentary Moana was filmed at Safune.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Savaiʻi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>When the Tripartite Convention of 1899 partitioned the Kingdom of Samoa, Germany received the western islands of Upolu and <b>Savaiʻi</b>, and the colony was proclaimed at Apia on 1 March 1900.</blockquote><a href="/state/german-samoa">German Samoa</a></li><li><blockquote>Samoan tradition holds that the god Tagaloa created the islands and that the first Tuʻi Manuʻa was his descendant, so the title predates the great titles of Upolu and <b>Savaiʻi</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-manu-a">Kingdom of Manuʻa</a></li></ul></section>
